# Facets: facet_wrap

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`facet_wrap()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/facet_wrap.html), in the order the page
gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot
draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of
what remains.

`Giggly.Plot.facet_wrap/3` splits a plot into panels, one for each value of a column, or each
combination of values of several, that occurs in the data, and wraps them into rows: a
one-dimensional sequence of panels laid out in two dimensions. Each panel draws the rows with its
values, and a strip above it says which those are. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn
with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the
cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with
`Kino.Image`. The data in these examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy
for 234 cars of 38 popular models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`, ggplot2's
name for it.

## Facets by one column

ggplot2 names the columns to facet by with `vars()`; Giggly names a column as a mapping does, by
an atom or a string.

```r
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) + geom_point()
# Use vars() to supply faceting variables:
p + facet_wrap(vars(class))
```

```elixir
plot =
  mpg
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

plot
|> Plot.facet_wrap(:class)
|> render.()
```

## The number of rows and columns

```r
# Control the number of rows and columns with nrow and ncol
p + facet_wrap(vars(class), nrow = 4)
```

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.facet_wrap(:class, nrow: 4)
|> render.()
```

## Facets by several columns

A panel for each combination of the columns' values that occurs, with a strip for each column.

```r
# You can facet by multiple variables
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(vars(cyl, drv))
```

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.facet_wrap([:cyl, :drv])
|> render.()
```

## How strips are labelled

```r
# Use the `labeller` option to control how labels are printed:
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(vars(cyl, drv), labeller = "label_both")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `labeller`, which here writes each strip as `cyl: 4` rather than `4`.

## The order of the panels

```r
# To change the order in which the panels appear, change the levels
# of the underlying factor.
mpg$class2 <- reorder(mpg$class, mpg$displ)
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(vars(class2))
```

Not in Giggly yet: choosing the order of a column's values. Giggly orders panels as it orders an
axis's levels, alphabetically, with a missing value last.

## Free scales

```r
# By default, the same scales are used for all panels. You can allow
# scales to vary across the panels with the `scales` argument.
# Free scales make it easier to see patterns within each panel, but
# harder to compare across panels.
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(vars(class), scales = "free")
```

```elixir
plot
|> Plot.facet_wrap(:class, scales: :free)
|> render.()
```

## Axes on every panel

```r
# When scales are constant, duplicated axes can be shown with
# or without labels
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(vars(class), axes = "all", axis.labels = "all_y")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `axes` and `axis.labels`, which draw an axis on every panel when the scales
are fixed.

## The same data in every panel

```r
# To repeat the same data in every panel, simply construct a data frame
# that does not contain the faceting variable.
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point(data = transform(mpg, class = NULL), colour = "grey85") +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(vars(class))
```

Not in Giggly yet: a layer with data of its own, and a fixed colour.

## Where the strips go

```r
# Use `strip.position` to display the facet labels at the side of your
# choice. Setting it to `bottom` makes it act as a subtitle for the axis.
# This is typically used with free scales and a theme without boxes around
# strip labels.
ggplot(economics_long, aes(date, value)) +
  geom_line() +
  facet_wrap(vars(variable), scales = "free_y", nrow = 2, strip.position = "top") +
  theme(strip.background = element_blank(), strip.placement = "outside")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `strip.position`, lines (`geom_line()`), dates, the `economics_long` dataset,
and changing the theme.

## The direction the panels fill

```r
# The two letters determine the starting position, so 'tr' starts
# in the top-right.
# The first letter determines direction, so 'tr' fills top-to-bottom.
# `dir = "tr"` is equivalent to `dir = "v", as.table = FALSE`
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  facet_wrap(vars(class), dir = "tr")
```

Not in Giggly yet: `dir` and `as.table`. Giggly's panels fill each row left to right, from the
top.
